KeyboardLights on Linux

Supports the legacy Cloud Nine C989 with firmware 1.38. Revised keyboards with
firmware 1.82 are incompatible.

Getting started

Reconnect the keyboard after installing or upgrading. Open KeyboardLights from
your application menu, or run keyboardlights as your normal desktop user.
Wait for "C989 connected" and "Current keyboard state loaded" in the footer.
If the keyboard is not connected or access is denied, unplug it, reconnect it,
and choose Refresh at the top right.

Making changes

Choose Solid color for fixed colors, or an animation and its speed. The pointer
selects keys without painting. Open the brush or bucket palette to choose a
color, then click keys. Clear starts a blank preview; Fill colors blank keys.
Paint or fill every blank key before saving or reviewing.

Select a key to edit its assignment below the keyboard. Choose Review changes,
check the summary, confirm, then choose Apply changes to update the keyboard.
If an update fails, the app stops and tells you what completed.

Profiles and preferences

File > Lighting Profile and File > Assignment Profile have New, Open, Save,
Save As, and Delete current actions. Opening a profile stages its settings;
review and apply them to update the keyboard.

Profile dialogs start in ~/.config/KeyboardLights/Profiles. Preferences and
optional logs use ~/.local/state/KeyboardLights. XDG_CONFIG_HOME and
XDG_STATE_HOME override those base directories. Removing or purging the package
keeps these files.

Help

Help > About shows the version and license. Run man keyboardlights for command
options. For diagnostic logs, close the app and relaunch it with:

    keyboardlights --diagnostic-logging

Logging is off by default. Relaunch without the flag to turn it off again.
